GOHF (34_one_health_framework) brings in technology mainly at its implementation step and in its surveillance and laboratory domains:
- software and web platforms, mobile phones, tablets and apps are used to implement surveillance, prevention, control and preparedness activities;
- in the United States, text-based monitoring improved detection of illnesses caused by novel influenza A viruses;
- smartphone apps: the WVS Data Collection app collects rabies data through its Integrated Bite Case Management system. The Kenya Animal Biosurveillance System (KABS) app expedites detection of wildlife and livestock zoonotic diseases. In 2017 KABS reported cattle mortalities that identified anthrax and triggered a One Health investigation;
- smartphone-based laboratory diagnostics: a system for detecting H5N1 avian influenza has "two-folder" higher detectability than traditional fluorescent strip readers;
- coordinated surveillance through interoperable platforms or information-sharing mechanisms gives early warning signals for emerging zoonotic disease and coordinated response. Standardized laboratory data shared across sectors speed outbreak detection.

The data-driven approach (37_data_driven_one_health) adds machine learning modelling capability to coordinated One Health surveillance:
- with data from preparedness, investigation and response, and mathematical model assumptions, the surveillance system can project disease progression and predict the magnitude and time of a potential outbreak;
- a cited machine learning model (Mollentze et al.) used viral and human genome sequence features to predict spillover. It correctly identified 70.8% of human viruses with high or very high zoonotic potential;
- big-data-driven machine learning and classical statistical methods would complement traditional surveillance. They would predict potential outbreaks at much lower economic cost than responding after the pathogens emerge.

Paper 45 (45_kenya_amr_digital) is about AMR, not zoonoses. Its digital platform, OHAMRS, has two parts: interoperability middleware and a DHIS2 web portal. It has 42 dashboards that update in near-real time. It aims to facilitate early detection, reporting and characterization of resistant pathogens.

GOHF incorporates technology at implementation: software and web platforms, mobile phones, tablets and apps support surveillance, prevention, control and preparedness [0]. Its examples are text-based monitoring for novel influenza A [1], and smartphone apps such as WVS for rabies data and KABS, which expedites detection of zoonotic diseases and triggered a One Health anthrax investigation [2, 3, 4, 5]. Smartphone-based laboratory diagnostics for H5N1 are another example [6, 7]. In its surveillance and laboratory domains, interoperable platforms and standardized data sharing give early warning signals, speed outbreak detection and support coordinated response [8, 9, 10]. The data-driven approach builds on One Health surveillance and data sharing for early detection and coordinated response [11] and adds machine learning modelling capability [12]. That capability is used to project disease progression and predict outbreak magnitude and timing [13]. It is also used to predict animal-to-human viral spillover, citing a model that correctly identified 70.8% of human viruses with high or very high zoonotic potential [14, 15]. Big-data machine learning and classical statistics would complement traditional surveillance and predict outbreaks at lower economic cost [16, 17]. In an AMR context rather than a zoonotic one, Kenya's OHAMRS integrates human and animal health data. It uses interoperability middleware, a DHIS2 web portal and 42 near-real-time dashboards to aid detection of resistant pathogens [18, 19, 20, 21, 22].
